cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use picasso, please cite it using the metadata below."
type: software
title: "picasso: self-contained Nix flakes that pin scientific, publishing and agentic toolchains"
abstract: >-
  picasso, formerly nix-config, is a set of self-contained Nix flakes, called labs, that pin
  scientific, publishing and agentic toolchains for other repositories. The ten labs are:
  WAVEWATCH III with gfortran, OpenMPI and NetCDF (pratico); a pandoc and TeX Live publishing
  pipeline with its own GitHub Action (publisher); Markdown to PDF for Portuguese in one Rust
  binary with Typst (ptbr-pdf); a lint set (lint); Markdown papers to PDF and arXiv source
  (paper); a Gleam toolchain for static sites (gleam-website); a sandbox and helpers for coding
  agents (agentic); a CUDA cache and PyTorch environment (cuda); a presenter's desk for screen
  annotation, capture and webcam profiles (present); and a code graph of a repository (viz). Each
  lab references nothing outside its own directory, commits its lock file and exports the same
  outputs, so a consumer gets the same pinned toolchain on any machine and in CI: the builds are
  deterministic, and CI builds every lab's checks in the Nix sandbox on every push. The code is
  only Nix and Rust: the repository tools are one Rust workspace and the lab tools are Rust
  crates. The smallest result is ptbr-pdf: the same Markdown-to-PDF job needs 729 MiB with pandoc
  and XeLaTeX and 25.2 MiB as one static Rust and Typst binary. picasso is the toolchain layer
  under WW3 GPU Lab, marola, ptbr-writer and other repositories, which borrow a lab as a flake
  input, a sparse git submodule or a GitHub Action.
